  "output": "I read through the actual codebase — `web/lib/server/*` (query layer), the conflict-queue client components, motion/WebGL components, and the `engine/` pipeline — rather than doing a generic sweep. Headline finding: **this codebase is already unusually disciplined about performance** — the DB layer batches deliberately, and every animated component cleans up correctly. So most of the standard checklist comes back clean; I'm reporting what's real, not padding it out.\n\n## 1. N+1 queries — none found in `web/`\n\n`web/lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `runs.ts`, `entities.ts`, `export.ts` are already written to avoid this. `getQueuePage` (conflicts.ts:172-200) does one query for rows, one for counts, one for total (`Promise.all`), then **one grouped** child-count query for the whole page (`countChildrenByParent`, entities.ts:28) — explicitly to avoid a query per row. Same pattern in `buildRunExport` and `getAuditPage`.\n\n**Forward-looking risk, not a current bug:** `engine/db.py` and `engine/pipeline/*` are 100% scaffolding right now — every function (`write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts`, `block`, `detect`, `resolve`) is `raise NotImplementedError(\"Phase 2/3 …\")`. Nothing to audit yet. When you implement them (Phase 2/3 per AGENTS.md), the N+1 risk will be:\n```python\n# DON'T — one INSERT per row for a run with thousands of entities\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    for e in entities:\n        conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO entities (...) VALUES (...)\", e.dict())\n\n# DO — psycopg3 executemany / COPY\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.executemany(\n            \"INSERT INTO entities (run_id, source_id, external_id, ...) VALUES (%s,%s,%s,...)\",\n            [(run_id, e.source_id, e.external_id, ...) for e in entities],\n        )\n```\nFlagging this now so it's a decision made once, not discovered after `eval/harness.py` is slow on a 500-account synthetic run.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — real, actionable\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`web/components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:58-63`) creates a **new closure per row on every render**:\n\n```tsx\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit => (action) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n...\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // new function identity every render\n```\n\nSame pattern in `ConflictDenseTable` (`submit(row.conflictId)` called inline per row) and `ConflictFocus`. None of the row components (`ConflictDiffRow`, `ListRow`, the dense-table row) are wrapped in `React.memo` — I grepped `web/components` for `memo(` and got zero matches. So even if you added `memo`, it wouldn't help: the props aren't stable.\n\n**Consequence:** navigating with `j`/`k` (a `selectedIndex` state change) or submitting one review (a `pendingId`/`decided` state change in `useReview`) re-renders **every row in the page** (up to 100, per `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE`) — including re-running `diffSegments` character-diffing for every value on every row. At the 400-conflict-queue scale AGENTS.md targets for keyboard-only review, this is the kind of thing that shows up as input lag, not a correctness bug.\n\n**Fix** — pass the stable `submit` from `useReview` straight down instead of wrapping it per row, and memo the row:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — drop submitFor entirely, pass submit + conflictId\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    submit={submit}              // stable reference from useReview\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  row, submit, pending, decidedStatus, selected, ...\n}: {\n  submit: (conflictId: string, action: ReviewAction) => Promise<ReviewResult>;\n  ...\n}) {\n  const runReview = (action: ReviewAction) => submit(row.conflictId, action);\n  // pass runReview to <ReviewActions submit={runReview} .../>\n  ...\n});\n```\n\nDo the same for the dense-table row (worth extracting it from the inline `.map()` into its own named component first — it's currently anonymous JSX inside `ConflictDenseTable`, which is itself why it can't be memoized) and `ListRow` in `ConflictFocus` (already a named component, just needs `memo`). With this, selecting row 47 only re-renders row 47 and whatever previously-selected row lost the `selected` prop — not the other 98.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- **The big one is already identified and not yet built.** `engine/agent/prompts.py:27-29` — `build_ruleset_block` raises `NotImplementedError`. AGENTS.md calls prompt caching on this block \"the single largest cost lever\" (it's identical across every call in a run). When you implement it, use Anthropic's `cache_control: {type: \"ephemeral\"}` breakpoint at the end of the ruleset block, before the per-conflict suffix — and route `eval/harness.py` sweeps through the Batch API as already specified, since those are never latency-sensitive.\n- `diffSegments` (`web/lib/diff-chars.ts`) is recomputed on every render for every visible value — O(n) on short strings, so low priority, but if the memo fix above still leaves it hot, wrap the call in `useMemo(() => diffSegments(value, against), [value, against])` inside `DiffValue`.\n- No other caching gap: the console is intentionally Server Components reading Postgres directly per AGENTS.md (\"everything else is a Server Component; do not reach for client fetching by default\"), so there's no stale-cache class of bug to introduce here — freshness is the point for a review queue.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every component with a timer/observer/RAF loop: `RunLive` (interval + `router.refresh`), `ConflictQueue` (keydown listener), `GradientWaves` (WebGL — cancels RAF, disconnects `ResizeObserver`, removes the `webglcontextlost` listener, calls `loseContext()`), `TextType` (`clearTimeout`), `LogoLoop` (`ResizeObserver.disconnect()`). All correctly clean up in their effect's return function. This is genuinely above the median for a project at this stage.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\nNothing significant beyond the per-render diff computation in #3. `getQueueCounts` (conflicts.ts:249) looks like it might double-query but it's actually two *different* grouped aggregates (by status, by severity), each computed with the opposite filter omitted — that's intentional, documented, and correct, not redundant.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Area | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| N+1 queries | Clean in `web/`. Engine unimplemented — noted a batching pattern to use when you build `db.py` writes. |\n| Re-renders | **Real issue** — memoize queue row components + stop recreating `submit` closures per row (fix above). |\n| Caching | Prompt caching on the ruleset block is the one that matters; it's Phase 3, not yet built. |\n| Memory leaks | None found. |\n| Redundant computation | Only the unmemoized `diffSegments` call, low priority. |\n\nWant me to apply the row-memoization fix to `conflict-diff-row.tsx`, `conflict-dense-table.tsx`, and `conflict-focus.tsx` now?\n",
